# Feature worktrees

When a task changes the site or a specific site area, work in a dedicated clean
Git worktree and feature branch.

1. Inspect `git status --short --branch` and `git worktree list` first.
2. Preserve unrelated changes in the current worktree; never reset, clean, or
   move them without explicit authorization.
3. Create the new worktree beside the repository, under
   `../3yeses-worktrees/<slug>`, with a branch named
   `feature/<area>-<slug>` based on the current `HEAD`:

   ```powershell
   git worktree add -b feature/<area>-<slug> ../3yeses-worktrees/<slug> HEAD
   ```

4. Verify the new worktree is clean before editing, then perform all feature
   work and tests there.
5. Report the branch and worktree path in progress updates and the final handoff.
6. Do not merge, delete the branch, or remove the worktree unless explicitly
   requested.
